From: electric-firebird
Subject: [PATCH] event.cpp: keep the process block in the primary mapping under Emscripten

Firebird delivered exactly one event under Emscripten and then never
again.  The watcher thread was blocked in eventWait forever while the
poster kept incrementing a counter it could not see.

SharedMemoryBase::mapObject() mmaps the shared-memory file a second
time, at the object's page-aligned offset, and returns a pointer into
that new mapping.  On a real OS both mappings are MAP_SHARED views of
the same pages, so a write through one is visible through the other.
Under Emscripten a second mmap of the same fd is a private copy:
measured directly — write 42 through the first mapping and the second
still reads 0.

EventManager caches m_process from mapObject, so it pointed into that
copy.  watcher_thread then mixed the two: it recomputed `process` from
SRQ_ABS_PTR (the real block) to clear the event and to test PRB_pending,
but waited on m_process->prb_event (the copy).  The first delivery
worked because PRB_pending was already set and it delivered before ever
waiting; from then on the poster advanced the real counter while the
watcher blocked on the copy's.  The destructor's shutdown wake-up posted
to the copy too, so teardown hung as well.

Firebird already supports building without object mapping, and that is
the correct configuration here: a single-process embedded engine has no
second process to map for, and the primary mapping is genuinely shared
between threads.  This takes that path under __EMSCRIPTEN__ only.

The watcher additionally waits on the pointer it just cleared rather
than on the cached one.  They are the same object after this change, but
that form is also the one that survives the region being remapped
underneath the loop — which is why the clear recomputes it every time.

Measured after: five POST_EVENTs from an AFTER INSERT trigger produce
five deliveries, with the engine's count advancing 1..5, and shutdown
completes.
---
diff --git a/src/jrd/event.cpp b/src/jrd/event.cpp
index 59b8139..e1844c4 100644
--- a/src/jrd/event.cpp
+++ b/src/jrd/event.cpp
@@ -103,7 +103,7 @@ EventManager::~EventManager()
 		(void) m_sharedMemory->eventPost(&m_process->prb_event);
 		m_cleanupSync.waitForCompletion();
 
-#ifdef HAVE_OBJECT_MAP
+#if defined(HAVE_OBJECT_MAP) && !defined(__EMSCRIPTEN__)
 		m_sharedMemory->unmapObject(&localStatus, &m_process);
 #else
 		m_process = NULL;
@@ -617,7 +617,14 @@ void EventManager::create_process()
 
 	m_processOffset = SRQ_REL_PTR(process);
 
-#ifdef HAVE_OBJECT_MAP
+// Emscripten: mapObject() mmaps the shared file a second time, and a second
+// MAP_SHARED mmap of one fd is a private copy there rather than an alias —
+// measured directly. Every use of m_process would then be reading and writing
+// memory no other thread touches: the watcher waited on a counter in the copy
+// while the poster advanced the real one, which is why exactly one event was
+// ever delivered. The primary mapping is genuinely shared between threads, and
+// a single-process engine has no second process to map for.
+#if defined(HAVE_OBJECT_MAP) && !defined(__EMSCRIPTEN__)
 	LocalStatus ls;
 	CheckStatusWrapper localStatus(&ls);
 	m_process = m_sharedMemory->mapObject<prb>(&localStatus, m_processOffset);
@@ -1335,7 +1342,11 @@ void EventManager::watcher_thread()
 			if (m_exiting)
 				break;
 
-			(void) m_sharedMemory->eventWait(&m_process->prb_event, value, 0);
+			// Wait on the pointer this iteration cleared rather than on the
+			// cached one. They are the same object now, but this form is also
+			// the one that survives the region being remapped underneath the
+			// loop — which is why the clear above recomputes it every time.
+			(void) m_sharedMemory->eventWait(&process->prb_event, value, 0);
 		}
 	}
 	catch (const Exception& ex)
